When looking for a lesser saturated red in LaTeX for use in color definitions, you might consider a lighter or softer shade of red. One common and easily recognizable name for a lesser saturated red is "Light Red" or "LightSalmon". However, LaTeX does not have a predefined color named "Light Red", but you can use "LightSalmon" or define a custom color with a lower saturation.

Here's how you can use "LightSalmon" in LaTeX:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{xcolor}

\begin{document}

This text is in \textcolor{LightSalmon}{LightSalmon}.

\end{document}
```

If you want to define a custom color with a lesser saturated red, you can use the `xcolor` package and define a new color with RGB values that have a lower saturation. For example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{xcolor}

\definecolor{myLightRed}{rgb}{1.0, 0.47, 0.47} % Lighter and less saturated red

\begin{document}

This text is in \textcolor{myLightRed}{myLightRed}.

\end{document}
```

In this example, `myLightRed` is a custom color with a lower saturation than a standard red. You can adjust the RGB values to get the exact shade you want.
